  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nThe 00Z HRRR near surface smoke density field is showing one last \nround of higher smoke densities rotating westward across our area \nthis morning, mainly affecting northern portions of the area. As the \nflow becomes more southerly through the day, the area of smoke will \nthen be pushed back off to our north through this afternoon. The \ndensity values are still not as high as they were late last week so \nwill generally just allow for some patchy smoke in the forecast \ntoday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nA seasonably strong mid level short wave will drop down across the \nwestern Great Lakes tonight and into the mid/upper Ohio Valley \nthrough the day on Tuesday. This will be accompanied by a 30-40 knot\n850 mb jet that will work its way southeastward across our area \nthrough the day. Meanwhile, southwest low level flow ahead of this \nwill lead to decent moisture advection up into the region with \nsurface dewpoints moving back into the low to possibly mid 70s. \n\nThere is some uncertainty with the potential for remnants of an MCS \ndropping down toward/into our area late tonight into Tuesday morning \nand what effects this could have on our temperatures through the day.\nAssuming that we are still able to recover enough from this, good \ninstability will develop through the afternoon. This would lead to \ndeveloping showers and thunderstorms, possibly initiating along a \nresidual boundary from the earlier MCS. The storms would then drop \nsoutheast across the remainder of our area through the afternoon, \npossibly into early evening. With seasonably strong deep and 0-3 km \nshear present, some severe storms would seem likely. Damaging wind \nremains the main threat but large hail and an isolated tornado would\nalso be possible. High PWs will also lead to the potential for \nlocally heavy rainfall/isolated flash flooding. Based on the timing,\nthe best chance for severe weather would likely be across about the \nsoutheast two thirds of our area through Tuesday afternoon/early\nevening.\n\nThe cold front will move southeast of the area Tuesday night, \nushering in a drier and cooler airmass for mid to late week.",
